  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1232 AM MDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nModels and ensembles are in much better agreement through\nFriday, with very wet conditions forecast along the Continental\nDivide for mid to late week. The latest trends for all guidance\nhave come up with QPF amounts, and now have the rainfall persist\nwell into Friday.\n\nTuesday...an embedded upper wave in the persistent southwest\nflow will lift north across Colorado Tuesday afternoon and\nevening. Moisture looks to be slightly increased, and\ninstability a bit higher for the Mountains and will lead to \nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms. The main threat\nwill be lightning. This activity will spread northeast during \nthe evening hours and come to an end overnight. High \ntemperatures on Tuesday will warm back into the lower to mid 70s\nacross the Plains, and overnight lows will remain mild in the \nupper 40s.\n\nWednesday through Friday...all model guidance and ensembles have\ncome into agreement with the evolution and track of an upper\nstorm system across Colorado. Deep tropical moisture, with PWAT\nvalues in excess of 0.8-1.2 inches are forecast to spread across\nsouthern Colorado Wednesday afternoon and continue into Friday.\nExpect showers and isolated thunderstorms to develop Wednesday\nafternoon along the Continental Divide, and remain persistently\nlift northward through this period. All signs are pointing\ntoward heavy rainfall. Most model guidance continues to come in\nwith higher QPF amounts, with areas around Wolf Creek Pass in\nthe 2-4+ inch range over this 3 day period. DESI probabilistic\nguidance has a greater than 90 percent probability of 2 or more\ninches over the San Juans, and around 40 percent for 4 inches.\nThe main concerns from this much rain will be flooding, and\nground may be unstable enough due to heavy rains over the past\nmonth to produce rock and mud slides. There is not a lot of cold\nair with this system, and snow levels will remain high, above\n12-13 kft through a majority of the storm. For the very high\npeaks of the San Juan Range and La Garita Mountains, snow could\nbe very heavy. It isn't until Thursday night into Friday morning\nthat snow levels may come down to near 10 kft, with only a\ncouple inches of slushy snow possible by the time precipitation\ncomes to an end. We will continue to monitor trends and the \npotential flooding possibilities.\n\nOn the Plains, not much in the way of precipitation will happen.\nSome guidance, including the GFS does force showers and isolated\nthunderstorms off the higher terrain and into the I-25 corridor\non Thursday. Looking at the QPF progs for the Plains, there has\nbeen very little consistency from run to run, and confidence\nthat areas along the I-25 corridor will see rain is low. East of\nI-25, looks to remain dry. The main concern on the Plains looks\nto be wind as the main upper trough lift northeast across the\nRockies. Most model guidance spreads 30-40 kt 700 mb flow across\nthe Plains Thursday afternoon and evening. Temperatures on the\nPlains will remain warm, with 70s to near 80 for mid to late\nweek.\n\nSaturday through Monday...models diverge significantly by next\nweekend with various trough and ridge positioning. The NBM\nguidance generally has low pops across the region and given the\nuncertainty, did not thinking changes were necessary.\nTemperatures will cool over the weekend with the passage of the\nupper trough on Friday and a cold front knocking highs back into\nthe 60s on the Plains.",
